Define a versioned, typed contract around user-visible behavior rather than database tables. A feed page should include stable item IDs, display data, reaction state, and an opaque continuation cursor; the response should say whether another page exists. Mutations need an idempotency strategy, a clear success representation, and structured errors that distinguish validation, authorization, conflict, and transient failure. Specify ordering, duplicate handling, deletion or moderation markers, timestamps, and whether reads are snapshot-consistent. Keep transport concerns separate from the view model through an adapter, so an API change does not spread through components. Agree on cacheability, invalidation, rate limits, and observability fields with backend owners, and test the contract with fixtures or generated types.

```ts caption="Keep the client contract explicit and independent of the persistence schema."
type FeedPage = {
  items: FeedItem[];
  nextCursor: string | null;
};
type ReactionResult = {
  itemId: string;
  reacted: boolean;
  count: number;
};
```

Treat each query as a request generation. On every new query, cancel the previous request when possible and capture the new `AbortSignal`; when a response arrives, also verify that its generation and query still match the current input. AbortController can abort fetch requests, response-body consumption, and streams, but cancellation is a resource and race-control aid, not a substitute for checking identity. Debounce only the remote work, not the input's visible value. Handle empty input separately, keep loading and error states accessible, and avoid announcing every keystroke to assistive technology. Cache normalized queries only when the data is safe and freshness is acceptable; show stale results only if the UI labels them and revalidation is clear.

```ts caption="Cancellation reduces obsolete work; the caller should still guard response identity."
let controller: AbortController | undefined;
async function load(q: string) {
  controller?.abort();
  controller = new AbortController();
  const response = await fetch(`/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`, {
    signal: controller.signal,
  });
  return response.json();
}
```

Separate translation messages, locale-sensitive formatting, and layout direction. Put all user-facing copy—including validation, empty, loading, and realtime status messages—behind message IDs with interpolation and plural rules; never concatenate translated fragments. Use `Intl` or an equivalent locale-aware layer for dates, numbers, lists, and currencies, and keep machine values distinct from formatted labels. Make layout logical rather than left/right-specific, support `dir` changes, and test expansion, truncation, long names, bidirectional text, and mixed-script content. Choose locale negotiation and persistence deliberately, and decide whether URLs, metadata, server rendering, and cache keys vary by locale. Load message data without delaying the critical path unnecessarily, but ensure a missing translation has a safe, observable fallback. Include translators and native-language reviewers in release acceptance.

```ts caption="Format values with the active locale instead of assembling punctuation by hand."
const label = new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, {
  style: "currency",
  currency: currencyCode,
}).format(amount);
```
